course: everyday
lesson: 294
title: Safe in the kitchen, near the freezer, with plugs and on balconies
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most accidents at home happen in ordinary places: the kitchen, the bathroom and near windows or balconies. Knowing why something is dangerous helps you avoid it even in a new situation.
explain: A sharp knife hidden under soapy bubbles is a trap: the next person to reach into the water cannot see the blade and gets cut. That is why knives are washed separately and put away at once.
explain: Water expands when it freezes. A can of soda left too long in the freezer can bulge and burst, and wet skin on very cold metal freezes and sticks to it.
explain: Water and mains electricity are a deadly pair. In the UK and Ireland, bathroom lights use pull-cord switches so wet hands never touch a switch, and ordinary sockets are not put in bathrooms.
explain: Example: a girl in New York leans over her balcony and wants to drop a small marble to see how fast it falls. From high up, even a small object can seriously hurt someone below, so nothing is ever dropped or thrown.
fact: Never leave a knife in soapy washing-up water, and never reach for one under the bubbles; wash knives separately and put them away at once.
fact: Before touching anything inside a blender, switch it off and unplug it; never put hands or a spoon in while it runs.
fact: The blades in a blender are very sharp and can start again if it is still plugged in.
fact: To reach a high cupboard, use a steady step stool, not a wheeled chair or the counter edge, or ask someone taller.
fact: Climbing on unsteady things is a common cause of falls at home.
fact: Roll up long sleeves and tie back long hair before cooking; loose cloth and hair can catch fire or drag a pan over.
fact: Walk, never run, in the kitchen: knives, hot pans and slippery floors make running dangerous.
fact: Never put your tongue on frozen metal, and let very cold ice lollies warm a little; wet skin freezes and sticks.
fact: Liquid expands when it freezes, so a can or glass bottle left in the freezer can burst.
fact: Never use mains electrical things such as hair dryers or chargers near a full bath or sink; a device falling into water can electrocute someone, which means give them a deadly electric shock.
fact: Plugs differ: three rectangular pins in the UK and Ireland, two flat pins in the USA and Canada, slanted pins in Australia and New Zealand; use a proper travel adaptor.
fact: Never throw or drop anything from a balcony; even a small object falling from a height can seriously hurt someone below.
exercise: You see the handle of a sharp knife sticking out of the soapy washing-up water. What is right? | Take it out carefully by the handle, wash it separately and put it away | Leave it there under the bubbles for the next person to find and wash | Push it down under the bubbles to soak | Feel around under the water for the blade
exercise: Why should a knife never be left under soapy bubbles? | Someone reaching into the water cannot see it and gets cut | Soap makes the knife go blunt at once, so it won't cut anything | The knife will rust in a minute | Bubbles make the water too hot
exercise: The blender is blocked with frozen fruit. What do you do first? | Switch it off and unplug it before touching inside | Poke the frozen fruit with a spoon while the blender is still running | Reach in quickly with your fingers | Shake the blender while it is running
exercise: Why must a blender be unplugged before you clear it? | The very sharp blades could start again | Unplugging makes the frozen fruit go softer | The blender gets cold if left plugged in | The plug may melt if it is left in too long
exercise: You want a snack from a high cupboard. What is safest? | Use a steady step stool or ask someone taller | Stand on a chair with wheels | Climb up onto the edge of the kitchen counter | Pile up some boxes and climb them
exercise: A boy in Ohio is about to stir a pot on a gas stove in a loose, long-sleeved jumper. What should he do first? | Roll up his sleeves and tie back long hair | Stir much faster, so that his sleeves don't have time to touch | Turn the flame up so it cooks quicker | Hold the sleeve in his mouth
exercise: A grown-up tells you the kitchen is not a place to run. Why? | Knives, hot pans and slippery floors make running there dangerous | Running makes the food taste worse | Kitchens are far too small for any games, and grown-ups need the space | Running makes the oven turn off
exercise: A girl wants to lick a metal ice-cube tray straight from the freezer. Why is that a bad idea? | Wet skin freezes to the metal and tears when pulled away | The metal will make all her teeth fall out by the next morning | Frozen metal is poisonous to touch | It will melt the ice too quickly
exercise: You put a can of soda in the freezer to cool it fast. What should you do? | Take it out soon, because it can burst as it freezes | Leave it in overnight so that it gets extra cold for the morning | Shake it first so it cools faster | Put it next to the freezer light
exercise: Why can a can burst in the freezer? | Liquid expands when it freezes | Cold makes the metal grow much bigger | The freezer adds gas into the can | Frozen soda gets lighter and floats
exercise: Your sister wants to dry her hair with a hair dryer while sitting on the edge of a full bath. What is true? | A device falling into the water can electrocute someone | Hair dryers are safe near water because they are plastic | Only the plug end can give a shock | It is fine if the dryer is on low
exercise: Why do bathrooms in the UK and Ireland have pull-cord light switches? | So wet hands never touch an electrical switch | Because they are much cheaper than buttons to buy and fit | So children cannot reach the light | Because the lights are too bright
exercise: On holiday in Australia, a family from Canada finds their plugs won't fit. What should they do? | Use a proper travel adaptor | Bend the pins to make them fit | Push hard until the plug goes in | Pull the socket cover off the wall
exercise: A boy on a tenth-floor balcony wants to drop a small stone to watch it fall. What is right? | Never drop anything, because it could seriously hurt someone below | It is fine if the stone is small | It is fine if he shouts a loud warning down to the street first | It is fine at night when it is dark
test: Your mum is washing up and says, «Just put your hand in and find the spoon». There may be a knife in there. What should you do? | Ask her to take the knives out first | Feel around fast so you don't get cut | Close your eyes and reach in really quickly | Stir the water around with your bare hand
test: The food processor stops with a carrot stuck in the blade. Which is the safe order? | Switch off, unplug, then remove the carrot | Remove the carrot first, then switch it off at the button | Leave it on and use a fork | Switch off but leave it plugged in
test: Which is the safest way to reach the top shelf of a bookcase? | A steady step stool | A spinning office chair | The arm of the big sofa | A wobbly pile of books
test: A girl with long hair leans over a candle on a birthday cake. What could happen? | Her hair could catch fire, so she should tie it back | Nothing, because hair does not burn and candles are too small | The candle would go out by itself | Only her eyebrows could be in danger
test: In Alaska in winter a boy wants to lick a frozen metal railing. What would happen? | His tongue could freeze and stick to the metal | His tongue would warm the rail and nothing happens | The rail would taste of ice cream | His tongue would turn blue for good
test: A glass bottle of juice is forgotten in the freezer overnight. What is likely? | It may crack because the juice expanded as it froze | It will shrink and get smaller overnight in the cold | The juice will turn back to fruit | Nothing, because glass cannot crack
test: Which is the safest place to charge a phone at home? | In a dry room away from water | On the edge of the bath | Beside a sink full of water | Next to a running shower
test: A boy from London visits New York and his three-pin plug won't fit the socket. Why? | Plugs differ between countries; he needs a travel adaptor | The New York socket must be broken and should be reported | He should push it in harder | American electricity has no plugs
test: A child on a fifth-floor balcony thinks dropping a grape is harmless. Which is TRUE? | Things falling from a height can hurt someone below, so drop nothing | A grape can never hurt anyone, it's soft and very light | Only heavy things fall fast | It is fine if nobody is looking
test: blender's jammed, ill poke it with a spoon | Switch it off and unplug it first | Poke it while it runs to free it up | Use your fingers, they're much gentler | Shake it upside down while it's on
test: can i lick the frozen pole for a dare | No, wet skin freezes and sticks to it | Yes, it melts as soon as you touch | Yes, if you're quick | Only with a warm tongue
test: put a can of fizzy in the freezer to cool it quick | Don't forget it, the drink expands and the can can burst | Fine, cans never burst in a freezer, they're made of strong metal | Fine, leave it overnight for best | Fine, glass bottles are even safer
test: dropping water balloons off the balcony on ppl lol | Don't, things falling from height can really hurt people | Fine, water balloons are soft and only make people wet | Fine if you shout first | Fine on the ground floor only
explain: A boy in Ohio reached into a blender to unstick a strawberry while it was still plugged in; it switched on and cut his fingers badly.
explain: A toddler in England climbed a chair someone had left by a balcony railing; she fell to the floor below and was badly hurt.
fact: Falls from windows and balconies badly hurt many young children every year, often after they climb on furniture.
fact: Blender and food processor blades can cut deep into fingers in a split second.
test: blenders stuck, just poke it with a spoon real quick | Switch off and unplug it first | Poke it while it's running | Poke it with your finger | Shake the blender upside down
test: chair next to the balcony rail, little cousin around | Move the chair away; toddlers climb and can fall | Leave it, she knows not to climb | Put a cushion on it | Tie her to the chair
test: can i lick the frozen pole for a dare | No, your tongue can stick and tear | Yes, it's just cold | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, if you blow on it first
test: did u hear a kid licked a frozen pole for a dare and "his tongue stuck, they had to pour warm water on it". what's the lesson | Never put your tongue on frozen metal; wet skin sticks | Only lick it if it's a bit warm out and the sun is shining on it | Rip your tongue off quickly | Use hot water before you lick it
test: my aunt says a kid on the 10th floor dropped a marble off the balcony and "it went through a car roof". what's the true bit | Even small things dropped from a height can seriously hurt someone | Marbles always break car roofs | Nothing small can hurt from a balcony | Only big heavy things are dangerous when dropped from a balcony
test: ppl say the boy who cut his finger in the blender is "so stupid". spread it? | No, don't mock him; always unplug a blender before touching it | Yes, he was stupid | Yes, and tell everyone how much blood there was all over the kitchen | Yes, it's a funny story
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the precaution required before touching anything inside a blender? | The appliance shall be switched off and unplugged | The appliance shall be switched to its lowest speed | The blades shall be cleared with a spoon while running | The lid shall be removed while it operates
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a glass bottle of liquid shall not be left in a freezer. | Liquid expands upon freezing and may burst the bottle | The glass absorbs the cold and grows soft enough to bend out of shape | Freezers are unable to cool glass | The liquid evaporates entirely
exam: It is given that a high cupboard is to be reached. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | A steady step stool shall be used, or someone taller asked | A wheeled chair shall be employed | The edge of the counter shall be climbed with care and without help | A stack of books shall be stood upon
exam: State the reason for which mains electrical devices are kept away from a full bath or sink. | A device falling into water may electrocute someone | Water damages only the device's appearance and not its workings | Steam drains the device's battery | Devices make bath water colder
